Fair correction — and you’re right. I was defining “harvest” as a research finding, but your stated goal was never to be a think tank. If the site is generating conversations with cable and inverter buyers, teaching you Python/GIS at speed, codifying your experience, and putting genuinely useful public tools into the world — that’s the return, and it’s already paying. The grid-opportunity finding is an option you hold, not a debt you owe.

On fault headroom — quick untangling, because it’s actually two different things people blur together. Thermal/firm capacity headroom is how much spare MW a substation or circuit can carry; fault level headroom is whether the switchgear can withstand the short-circuit current that a new connection (especially inverters or rotating plant) would add — a substation can have spare MW but no fault headroom, and vice versa. And here’s the good news on confidentiality: the headline numbers are public by design. The DNOs are obliged to publish network capacity/heat maps and Embedded Capacity Registers (UKPN, NGED, SSEN, SPEN, NPg all do — demand and generation headroom per substation, often with fault level data in their long-term development statements), and NESO’s TEC register and DFES scenarios are open too. What’s confidential is project-specific: individual connection offers, customer details, and the detailed fault studies behind an offer. So mapping published headroom against your REPD pipeline layers is entirely legitimate — it’s literally what the publications are for.

That could slot into Atlas V8 one day as just another GeoJSON layer, on your one-layer-at-a-time rule. But only when it serves the actual mission — leads and education — not because an AI suggested the grid needed finding.
